; TEST: expect-exit=100
;
; `ordinata` over integers -- the same definition as the float case
; (red_ordinata_f32.ir), with the integer group's identities and the integer
; group's TRAPS.
;
;   1  add i64,  contributions [1, 2, 3, 4]      = 10    (identity 0)
;   2  sub i64,  contributions [1, 2, 3]         = -6    ((0-1)-2)-3
;   3  add i64,  contributions [-5, 5]           = 0     signs fold
;   4  add u8,   contributions [100, 100, 55]    = 255   at the very top of
;                                                        u8, and NOT trapping
;   5  add i64,  no contribution at all          = 0     the identity
;
; CHECK 4 IS THE ONE THAT MATTERS FOR THE CANONICAL FORM. Each fold is the
; standalone `add u8` -- emit.inc routes the accumulate step through
; __bfa_emit_addsub_core, the same routine the dispatcher's `add` reaches --
; so the running total is normalised and checked at u8 after EVERY
; contribution, not once at the end. 100 + 100 = 200 and 200 + 55 = 255 both
; fit; a fourth contribution of 1 would trap (red_arborea_int_trap.ir is
; that case in the other shape). An accumulator held at 64 bits and
; normalised only by `redfin` would also answer 255 here -- and would answer
; 300 & 0xFF instead of trapping on overflow, which is why the trap fixture
; exists beside this one.
;
; CHECK 2 fixes what `sub` means as a reduction: fold from the identity, so
; ((0-1)-2)-3 = -6, the negated sum. See red_ordinata_f32.ir's header for the
; argument that this is the definition rather than a choice of seed.
;
; Mutation (run): the accumulator seeded with the first contribution -> exit
; 2 (`sub` gives 1-2-3 = -4). The per-fold normalisation dropped -> exit 4
; survives but red_arborea_int_trap.ir stops trapping.
;
; CROSS-CHECKED THROUGH THE C BACKEND, 2026-09-27. This fixture carried
; `c-emit-exit=4` from the day it landed: the C backend refused the three
; reduction opcodes, so the differential phase could only check parity of a
; refusal. It carries the key no longer -- `backend_c/emit_c.inc` lowers
; `redinit`/`contrib`/`redfin`, and the four builds (gcc and clang, -O0 and
; -O2, every one under `-fsanitize=undefined -fno-sanitize-recover=all`) are
; held to the SAME directive above as the reference's own run. The reference's
; verdict here is unchanged and has been since the day it landed; what is new
; is that three more toolchains have to agree with it.
;
; What this one cross-checks in particular: that the C fold is the standalone
; opcode there too. `add i64` becomes `exsi_add_i(acc, v, 64)` and `add u8`
; becomes `exsi_add_u(acc, v, 8)` -- the same prologue helpers
; `__bfc_emit_bin` picks for a standalone `add`, so check 4's 255 is
; normalised and checked at u8 after every contribution in both backends, by
; the same routine in each.

functio @initium (ptr) -> u8 {
b0:
%0 = param ptr 0
%1 = iconst i64 1
%2 = iconst i64 2
%3 = iconst i64 3
%4 = iconst i64 4
%5 = redinit i64 add ordinata 0
contrib %5 %1
contrib %5 %2
contrib %5 %3
contrib %5 %4
%6 = redfin i64 %5
%7 = iconst i64 10
%8 = cmp.eq i64 %6 %7
br %8 b2 b1
b1:
%9 = iconst u8 1
ret %9
b2:
%10 = redinit i64 sub ordinata 0
contrib %10 %1
contrib %10 %2
contrib %10 %3
%11 = redfin i64 %10
%12 = iconst i64 -6
%13 = cmp.eq i64 %11 %12
br %13 b4 b3
b3:
%14 = iconst u8 2
ret %14
b4:
%15 = iconst i64 -5
%16 = iconst i64 5
%17 = redinit i64 add ordinata 0
contrib %17 %15
contrib %17 %16
%18 = redfin i64 %17
%19 = iconst i64 0
%20 = cmp.eq i64 %18 %19
br %20 b6 b5
b5:
%21 = iconst u8 3
ret %21
b6:
%22 = iconst u8 100
%23 = iconst u8 55
%24 = redinit u8 add ordinata 0
contrib %24 %22
contrib %24 %22
contrib %24 %23
%25 = redfin u8 %24
%26 = iconst u8 255
%27 = cmp.eq u8 %25 %26
br %27 b8 b7
b7:
%28 = iconst u8 4
ret %28
b8:
%29 = redinit i64 add ordinata 0
%30 = redfin i64 %29
%31 = cmp.eq i64 %30 %19
br %31 b10 b9
b9:
%32 = iconst u8 5
ret %32
b10:
%33 = iconst u8 100
ret %33
}
